/* (twentieth pass, J2, before the third working sheet: cell 1.6, thin 0.12 to 0.4, run 2 to 13 and most of them short, back 0.24 before) THE GREEN RUNS DOWN IN STREAKS 0.6 to 1.6 m broad and 5 to 18 m long (short narrow ticks under the lip before: drips, slit windows); the metal in the shade lighter (back 0.38) */ const BRONZE = { back: 0.08 /* (n3, stage L, L1b: 0.38 before) BRONZE IN SHADE IS DARK, NEVER ORANGE: the warm light thrown back onto the shaded metal stood as an orange band on her shade side; its mirror (MIRROR) lights it now, with the dim sky */, turn: 0.3, lee: 0.5, lo: [0.1, 0.062, 0.03], hi: [0.24, 0.155, 0.06], lit: 0.36, streak: [0.36, 0.72], verd: [[0.05, 0.66, 0.41], [0.07, 0.82, 0.51]] /* (n6l2: 0.16, 0.48, 0.34 to 0.32, 0.72, 0.5 before) A BRIGHTER VERDIGRIS: through the pink air of 200 m and more the old one read green only from 0.7 of a pixel's cover, this one from 0.3 */, broad: 0.24, pale: 0.12, cell: 3, thin: [0.13, 0.17], run: [3, 30] /* (n6l2: cell 9, thin 0.08 to 0.16, run 0.6 to 1.5 and share 0.8 at first, then cell 5, thin 0.06 to 0.1 and share 0.7: the green was as good as gone from the wall at the cliff's foot) THIN GREEN STREAKS DOWN FROM UNDER THE COPING, UNEVEN IN LENGTH: one in a cell of 3 m, 0.8 to 1 m broad at its head and narrowing, 3 to 30 m long, 0.9 of the cells in groups; never a sheet, never a blotch */ /* (n6l: thin 0.26 to 0.5 and run 8 to 27 before) THE GREEN IS GATHERED UNDER THE COPING AND THE LEVEL SEAMS IN SHORT RUNS: each under a metre and a half long and about a metre broad at its head (2 to 4.5 m broad and 8 to 27 m long before: broad green sheets and blotches on the metal) */, hold: 0.4, share: 0.9 };      /* (r21, STAGE H, H4, the lead's order: LET THE BROAD VERDIGRIS AND THE LIGHT TELL THE METAL, green streaks plainly seen from 150 to 300 m) SEEN on r21g, enlarged: the green stood as a few pale grey-blue smears under the lip. Now GREENER AND LESS PALE (verd 0.2, 0.5, 0.47 to 0.4, 0.76, 0.7 before: a blue green that the low sun's orange turns grey; pale 0.25), BROADER (cell 9 m and thin 0.26 to 0.5 of it: 3 to 6 m broad at the head; 6 m and 0.2 to 0.62: over half a cell a streak was cut by its cell's edge, an upright line), and each keeps its strength over the first hold 0.4 of its run (0.15: most of a streak was faint) */      /* (r21, stage F, F4) WEATHERED: the metal darker and browner (lo 0.13, 0.085, 0.04 and hi 0.3, 0.21, 0.085 before), THE GREEN IN MORE AND LONGER STREAKS FROM THE RIM (cell 7.5, thin 0.16 to 0.5, run 4 to 22, share 0.5 before) and on her lee side at half its strength (lee 0.25) */      /* (r21, A2) the green's streaks fewer, unequal and farther apart (cell 3.2, share 0.75 before: one every 3 m, an upright rhythm with the laps) */      /* (twentieth pass, C 3) cell, thin, run, share: the verdigris in streaks from the rim, see the shader's stain (streak and broad are read no more). (the same pass, by a plain drawing of the stain's law in node before any sheet: scratchpad p20/bronzeview.mjs) THE STREAKS STOOD AS A FRINGE OF SHORT PALE SPIKES UNDER THE RIM (icicles): run 2 to 13 m (1.5 to 9), soft at their sides and thinning away from a fifth of their length on (whole to the half before), and pale 0.25 (0.5: the green was lifted to near white on the dark metal) */      /* (after the working sheet: pale 0.9 before: the streaks stood as pale panels) */      /* (nineteenth pass, B) VERDIGRIS IN BROAD COLD STREAKS, to be seen at 300 m: each some 4 to 5 m broad (BRONZE.broad: its noise's turns to the metre; 0.75 before, a metre or two), colder and lighter (verd), and matt: lit BRONZE.pale more than the metal's own dark */      // (sixteenth pass, K1 b) BRONZE FROM AFAR: its own colour a warm golden brown between lo and hi, lit at BRONZE.lit of the sun and the sky (0.3 before, on a dark red-brown: near black far off); the verdigris ONLY IN STREAKS under the lip and the hoops (streak: the thresholds of its noise along the wall; broad soft stains before), of a bluer, lighter green (verd)
const UNDERCUT = { zig: 0, zigEvery: 5.3, fade: 4, tuck: 0.6, reach: 11, lip: [1.6, 3.8], swing: [2.4, 1.7], wave: [6.3, 2.3], own: 0.2, deep: [0, 0], round: 2.4, rag: 0.6, run: 37, dark: 0.6, east: { x: [-900, -899], deep: [3.6, 6.4] }, least: 1 };      /* (r21, D1) NOT THE WHOLE WAY ROUND ANY MORE: the notes below are the passes' own; since r21 D the turn dies west of her x = KEEL.west[1] and is none west of KEEL.west[0], and east of KEEL.x[0] it rises into her keel (KEEL, the next line but one) */      /* (twentieth pass, G1, after the first working sheet: lip [2.7, 3.0], round 0.6, rag 0.3 before: one level under edge all round, cut in sharply, each joint's cell at its own level and depth: a ruled slab on squarish blocks) A RAGGED BILGE OF UNEQUAL DEPTH: the height at which her foot turns in wanders between lip[0] and lip[1] m of her own height along the coast (the sine of two noises of wave[0] and wave[1] m, swing each, so that it rests at no height, and a rise and fall of UNDERCUT.zig m every UNDERCUT.zigEvery m besides (0: tried at 0.45 m, no gain by count and the sea met her rock at eight columns at her lowest); a joint's cell adds only own of it), and the turn is eased over round m of height, as a hull's turn of bilge: the lip is measured from the level sea at her sunk (west) side and in her own heights at her lifted side, so that with her list the turn stands clear of the sea the whole way round (in her own heights alone her west side went into the sea as a wall: by count 180 of 502 columns on seed 7) and rises with her at her lifted end, as a listing hull shows its bilge; UNDER THE TURN HER BODY GOES ON IN AND DOWN (UNDERCUT.tuck m further in for every metre under the turn, unevenly, over UNDERCUT.reach m: under the turn it stood plumb before, 3.5 m inside the edge from the turn to the sea: at her lifted end a wall 8 m high under a slab, a footing)) */      /* (twentieth pass, A 1) least over 0: under her lip the rock stands back east.deep[0] m and more from THE INNERMOST of her own face just over the lip, at every column (a bed that stood proud at the sea under a bed set back over the lip ate the turn: by count 335 of 20,080 columns of her foot on forty seeds) */      /* (twentieth pass, A 1: lip [-5.6, -4.2], east.x [38, 88], east.deep [2.5, 5.5] before: the turn at her lifted east end alone) HER FOOT TURNS UNDER LIKE A BILGE THE WHOLE WAY ROUND: right under her belt's foot (lip, her own heights) the rock turns in and goes back under her east.deep m, unevenly, on every side (east.x lies west of all of her: the 'east' law is the law all round); the level sea cuts her 3 to 6 m inside that edge, in her own shade */      /* (nineteenth pass, A 3, the owner's yes) AT THE LIFTED END THE FOOT IS UNDERCUT: from her own height UNDERCUT.lip (just under the weed's belt) down, east of her x = east.x[0] (eased to east.x[1]), the rock is cut back east.deep m, unevenly: where the list holds that end 4 to 11 m out of the sea the belt overhangs and dark water lies in under it. Nowhere else (deep [0, 0]): no plinth. (No undercut anywhere before.) */      /* (eighteenth pass, A) NOT A PLINTH: NO UNDERCUT AND NO SET-BACK ANYWHERE (deep [3, 8], dark 0.55, east deep [1, 2], least 2.5 before: a darker, narrower, square-cut footing under an overhang): the under-body is the cliff's own stone going down, by the cliff's one maker, flush with the faces above */      // (seventeenth pass, L6) at the lifted east end (the island's x from east.x[0] to east.x[1] and on) the cut is east.deep m deep and not dark: the wet band stands in the light there; everywhere else no foot stands less than UNDERCUT.least m back from the innermost of its own face above the lip
//      // (sixteenth pass, K3 b) THE FOOT OVERHANGS THE SEA: under a lip lip[0] to lip[1] m of the island's own height the rock is cut back inward deep[0] to deep[1] m, unevenly along the coast (over run m), the turn under the lip round m high; dark: the shade in it. deep [0, 0] restores the foot without it
const KEEL = { x: [9000, 9001] /* (r21, stage F, F1, the lead's order) THE KEEL IS OUT: a stranger read her lifted east end as a fault (undercut and hovering, a dark shelf over the water). SHE SITS IN THE SEA ALL ROUND: x and west lie beyond her (her x never reaches them: [48, 104] and [48, 80] until stage E), so no turn of her foot stands anywhere, her body is drawn in nowhere, no water falls and no weed hangs from a turn; the measures after these and every note on the keel below are stages D and E's own and build nothing now */, rise: 15, c: [80, -128], in: 0.92, curve: 0.8, bow: 1.08, tuck: 0.15, west: [9000, 9001], fall: { from: 0.5, every: [5, 11], sheets: 0.7, thread: [0.3, 0.7], sheet: [5, 11], under: 0.3, tone: 0.9, gain: [0.7, 0.75] }, hang: { from: 0.3, every: [0.6, 1.4], len: [1.0, 3.4], w: [0.4, 1.0], tone: [0.03, 0.04, 0.022] }, wet: 0.8, weed: 0.6, shell: 0.3, dark: 0.3, glow: [0.01, 0.028, 0.034] } /* (r21, stage E, E1 sight 4) HER LIFTED EAST END IS PLAINLY CLEAR OF THE SEA: rise 15 (11.5 in stage D) and in 0.92 (0.62): on seed 7 the turn stands 16.6 to 18.8 m over the level sea where her keel is whole and her body is drawn in 25 to 46 m at the sea, to a pointed waterline under her end, so that from the line (120 m and more off) her end overhangs the water as a ship's bow and the far sea's light stands under it. THE WATER FALLS FROM IT IN VEILS: fall.sheets 0.7 of the falls are sheets fall.sheet 5 to 11 m broad (torn sheets of 2 to 4 m and threads in stage D: from the line they read as strings), each a soft white body in metre-broad strands (DRIP_FRAG), thinning downward; the stage D figures in the notes after this one are that stage's own. */ /* fall: the water that falls from her keel (dealt where keelK is fall.from and more, one every fall.every m of her coast; fall.sheets of them torn sheets fall.sheet m broad, the rest threads fall.thread m broad; fall.under of them from her rock further in under the turn; tone and gain: its light and its strength, a thread's and a sheet's); hang: the tassels of weed at the turn's edge (from keelK hang.from, one every hang.every m, hang.len m long at the full keel, hang.w m broad, hang.tone); wet, weed, shell, dark: the paint of her rock that looks down at the sea under the turn (dark wet stone, weed on it, pale shell in patches, and how much of the light thrown up from the sea it loses: it stands in its own shade) */, keelK = (x) => sstep(KEEL.x[0], KEEL.x[1], x), keelW = (x) => sstep(KEEL.west[0], KEEL.west[1], x);      /* (r21, D1, the lead's order) HER KEEL. SHE LISTS DOWN BY THE WEST, AND HER LIFTED EAST END SHOWS HER KEEL AS A SHIP'S FOREFOOT: by HER OWN x alone, the same on every seed and from every place: east of x = KEEL.x[0] the turn of her foot BEGINS AND RISES (it is whole from x = KEEL.west[1]; KEEL.rise m higher at x = KEEL.x[1] and east of it: 11.1 to 13.3 m over the level sea by count on forty seeds; 7.8 to 10 m before the lead's word that the keel be BOLD), and under the turn her body is drawn in toward one point of her own (KEEL.c, in the plan's metres) by KEEL.in of the way at the sea (a ship's bow drawn in to its stem: 16.5 to 31.9 m by count on forty seeds where the keel is whole, her x 104 and more; 13 to 25 m before; the share grows from the turn down to the sea as the height's own power KEEL.curve, and goes on to KEEL.bow of it under the sea), so that the sea runs in unbroken under her lifted end and the far horizon's light stands in a slit between her turned foot and the water wherever her east end is seen from the side; under it the rock goes back only KEEL.tuck m a metre along its own outward side (UNDERCUT.tuck elsewhere). WEST OF x = KEEL.west[0] THERE IS NO TURN AT ALL (r21, D1 bold: no thin dark fringe along her south, west and north coasts; from x -72 to 60 a turn 1.6 to 3.8 m over the sea ran before): the rock goes down into the sea. The sea's light on the rock that looks down under the keel: KEEL.glow. She heaves and rolls on the world clock (HEAVE_M, ROLL_DEG), so the slit opens and narrows with her ride; nothing of it reads the eye, a still, a seed's number or a clock of its own */
